I wonder about that based on monster strategy.  Monsters will
shoot wands when those are in range, wield bows when arrows
are in range, throw daggers, wield polearms when those are in
range, wield melee weapons while adjacent.  That's a lot of
turns spent switching wielded weapon.  Sure enough monsters
drop like flies but I've never considered that this suboptimal
strategy contributes much to why they die so readily.  I think
they mostly die because they are all willing to fight to the death
every single time so they never retreat to heal until you get to
the very mean teleporting named demons.

Fighting with every ranged attack you have is definitely a good
strategy to follow for monsters.  It's a good thing they aren't
smart enough to hang back and use up every wand before
closing range.  It's a good thing they continue to advance
while firing arrows rather than hanging back until they run out
of arrows.  My players often fire missiles while retreating and
often empty their quiver before closing for melee.

Being able to fight with a wielded weapon and also "f"ire
daggers without a wasted turn is a tremendous advantage:

At least once I've faced the Castle drawbridge with a wielded
bow and a big quiver of arrows.  Round after round of "f"iring
arrows as monster after monster dies on the bridge, but if even
one monster survives the barrage of arrows it makes it off the
bridge onto the square where the bridge lands and has room to
move around me.  It seems like there is always a monster that
makes it (never an easy monster of course) while there are still
plenty of monsters still in the Castle waiting for room on the
bridge to get out.  The bow works great at first but as soon as
the first monster survives like that it's time to switch to a melee
weapon and there's no longer time to switch back to arrows.

Doing that with a samurai I've then switched to "f"iring daggers
until my quiver of daggers empties without wasting a turn
switching wielded weapons.  I never seem to carry enough
daggers to make a significant dent in the monsters filing across
the bridge.  I never seem to carry enough arrows to take out all
of the monsters filing across the bridge.  Even with both I tend
to melee some monsters before entering the Castle.

Thinking this over maybe the next time I'm at the Castle bridge
with a samurai or ranger, I'll move forward to the square the
bridge comes down on as soon as it comes down.  Then start
a barrage of missile fire on the advancing monsters.  That
requires at least reflection and preferably both reflection and MR.
A better way to keep control of advancing monsters equals more
exposure to wand danger, funny how that works.  ;^)


